{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run/draft && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nclaim={\\\"id\\\":\\\"C8-timeout-enqueues-blocked-instance\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"obligation\\\",\\\"concern\\\":\\\"bounded_liveness\\\",\n \\\"description\\\":\\\"When a replica's executor finds an instance it cannot apply (no commands, not committed) at the head of a row, it must enqueue that instance for recovery once the configured grace period has elapsed, so the row does not stay blocked on that replica.\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"S4-execute-loop\\\",\\\"S14-recovery-start-prepare\\\",\\\"S23-state-conflict-apply\\\"],\n \\\"scope\\\":{\\\"description\\\":\\\"One replica whose row 1 is blocked at instance 0 by a record with no commands and no commit, with the executor goroutine running as in the launched configuration.\\\",\n  \\\"assumptions\\\":[\\\"crtInstance for the row is at the blocked instance and the executed watermark is behind it\\\",\\\"no other instance in the row is ready to apply\\\"],\n  \\\"excluded\\\":[\\\"what the recovery that follows does with the instance (other claims cover the resolution and its consequences)\\\",\\\"the client-visible effect of the delay\\\",\\\"rows whose head is ready\\\"]},\n \\\"pending\\\":[],\n \\\"grounding\\\":{\\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"S4-execute-loop\\\"],\\\"expectation_ids\\\":[],\\\"binding_ids\\\":[\\\"BB9-timeout-trigger\\\"],\n  \\\"derivation\\\":\\\"executeCommands breaks out of a row at the first instance it cannot apply, accumulates the grace period in that row's timeout counter and, when the counter reaches COMMIT_GRACE_PERIOD, pushes the whole suffix from the blocked instance into instancesToRecover. This is the path that lets a replica that missed an instance make progress without a client stimulus, so the duty is that the enqueue happens, not that recovery succeeds.\\\",\n  \\\"applicability\\\":\\\"Applies to any replica whose executor is running with a blocked row head; the launched configuration starts executeCommands whenever Exec is true.\\\",\n  \\\"unresolved\\\":[\\\"the grace period is measured in loop iterations of one millisecond each in this implementation, so the wall-clock wait is an observation rather than a specification\\\"],\\\"conflicts\\\":[],\\\"alternatives\\\":[]}}\nb9={\\\"id\\\":\\\"BB9-timeout-trigger\\\",\\\"material_id\\\":\\\"S4-execute-loop\\\",\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"executeCommands\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":385,\\\"end_line\\\":433,\n \\\"description\\\":\\\"Executor loop and its per-row timeout: a blocked instance accumulates the grace period and is enqueued to instancesToRecover when it elapses.\\\",\n \\\"associations\\\":[{\\\"claim_id\\\":\\\"C8-timeout-enqueues-blocked-instance\\\",\\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"S4-execute-loop\\\",\\\"S14-recovery-start-prepare\\\"],\n   \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"The branch that enqueues the suffix is the mechanism the claim asserts; the enqueued instance is what startRecoveryForInstance later consumes.\\\"}],\n \\\"pending\\\":[]}\nplan={\\\"description\\\":\\\"Discriminator: with row 1 blocked at instance 0 by a record that has no commands and is not committed, does the executor enqueue that instance for recovery? Prefix: the harness prepares the blocked record with the package's own constructor, records the row watermarks in a prerequisite event, and starts the real executeCommands goroutine. Controls: the prerequisite prints the record's status, whether it has commands, crtInstance and ExecedUpTo, and the result prints whether the channel received the instance plus the wall-clock wait as a diagnostic.\\\",\n \\\"claim_id\\\":claim[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"binding_ids\\\":[\\\"BB9-timeout-trigger\\\"],\n \\\"harness\\\":{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"go_test\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"In-package Go test that blocks one row and waits for the executor's recovery enqueue, reading only the recovery channel.\\\",\n  \\\"semantic_changes\\\":[\\\"harness-only scaffolding: the Replica is constructed in-package and executeCommands is started directly instead of by New(); the loop itself is unmodified\\\",\\\"the harness reads instancesToRecover rather than letting the run loop consume it\\\"],\n  \\\"prerequisites\\\":[{\\\"alias\\\":\\\"blocked_instance\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"blocked_instance_prepared\\\",\\\"conditions\\\":[{\\\"field\\\":\\\"status\\\",\\\"value\\\":0},{\\\"field\\\":\\\"has_commands\\\",\\\"value\\\":False}]}],\n  \\\"legality\\\":{\\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"S4-execute-loop\\\"],\\\"expectation_ids\\\":[],\\\"binding_ids\\\":[\\\"BB9-timeout-trigger\\\"],\n   \\\"derivation\\\":\\\"The blocked record is the state a replica reaches when it has an instance number but no committed commands for it, and the loop is the implementation's own executor.\\\",\\\"applicability\\\":\\\"One replica, one blocked row, executor running.\\\",\\\"unresolved\\\":[\\\"the wall-clock length of the grace period is an observation because the counter advances per loop iteration\\\"],\\\"conflicts\\\":[],\\\"alternatives\\\":[]}},\n \\\"monitors\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"M1-recovery-enqueued\\\",\\\"checker_id\\\":\\\"CH1-recovery-enqueued\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"recovery_enqueued\\\",\\\"binding_ids\\\":[\\\"BB9-timeout-trigger\\\"],\n   \\\"grounding\\\":{\\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"S4-execute-loop\\\"],\\\"expectation_ids\\\":[],\\\"binding_ids\\\":[\\\"BB9-timeout-trigger\\\"],\n    \\\"derivation\\\":\\\"The event reports whether the instance arrived on the recovery channel and which instance it was, which is the duty's observable.\\\",\\\"applicability\\\":\\\"The row was blocked and the executor was running.\\\",\\\"unresolved\\\":[],\\\"conflicts\\\":[],\\\"alternatives\\\":[]},\n   \\\"applicability_conditions\\\":[{\\\"field\\\":\\\"instance\\\",\\\"reference\\\":\\\"blocked_instance.instance\\\"}],\\\"admission_alias\\\":\\\"blocked_instance\\\"}],\n \\\"observable_properties\\\":[{\\\"checker_id\\\":\\\"CH1-recovery-enqueued\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"event_assertion\\\",\\\"trigger\\\":{\\\"field\\\":\\\"event\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"recovery_enqueued\\\"},\n   \\\"assertion\\\":{\\\"field\\\":\\\"enqueued\\\",\\\"value\\\":True},\\\"identity_fields\\\":[\\\"instance\\\"],\n   \\\"description\\\":\\\"A blocked instance must be enqueued for recovery once the executor's grace period elapses.\\\"}],\n \\\"uncertainties\\\":[\\\"the check observes the enqueue, not the recovery it triggers\\\",\\\"the grace period's wall-clock length depends on loop iterations, so the measured wait is a diagnostic rather than a specification\\\"]}\nq={\\\"audit_spec_version\\\":10,\\\"disposition\\\":\\\"ready_for_check\\\",\\\"preferred_check\\\":\\\"direct_test\\\",\n \\\"question\\\":\\\"With one row blocked at an instance whose record has no commands and is not committed, does the executor enqueue that instance for recovery after its grace period, and roughly how long does that take in wall-clock terms?\\\",\n \\\"importance\\\":\\\"This is the path that lets a replica which missed an instance make progress without any client stimulus, and it is the alternative the dead rejection-path finding (C7) relies on: without the enqueue, a rejected accept round would block the row indefinitely. Establishing it as a protection also measures the delay a stalled row actually incurs.\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"S4-execute-loop\\\",\\\"S14-recovery-start-prepare\\\",\\\"S23-state-conflict-apply\\\",\\\"S12-handleAcceptReply\\\",\\\"S15-handlePrepareReply\\\",\\\"S20-exec\\\"],\n \\\"contexts\\\":[\\\"row blocked at instance 0 by a record with no commands and no commit\\\",\\\"executor goroutine running as in the launched configuration\\\"],\n \\\"event_paths\\\":[\\\"executeCommands finds the blocked instance at the head of the row\\\",\\\"the per-row timeout counter accumulates the grace period\\\",\\\"the suffix is pushed into instancesToRecover\\\"],\n \\\"activity_classes\\\":[\\\"A2\\\"],\\\"behavior_ids\\\":[\\\"B9-recovery-trigger\\\"],\\\"fact_ids\\\":[\\\"F3-execution-prefix\\\"],\n \\\"supporting_behavior_ids\\\":{\\\"B7-execute-scheduler\\\":\\\"the executor loop whose timeout branch is under test\\\",\\\"B11-prepare-resolution\\\":\\\"what happens to the instance once recovery starts\\\"},\n \\\"obligation_relation_kind\\\":\\\"recovery\\\",\\\"priority\\\":2,\n \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\\\"scoped to the source: the loop breaks at the first instance it cannot apply and, when the row's counter reaches COMMIT_GRACE_PERIOD, pushes every instance from the blocked one to crtInstance into instancesToRecover (S4-execute-loop)\\\",\n   \\\"scoped to the run: the local construction run reports the prerequisite (status 0, no commands, crtInstance 0, ExecedUpTo -1) and then enqueued true for instance 1.0 after a wait of about 12.6 seconds, which is the built-in one-millisecond-per-iteration counter reaching its limit\\\"],\n \\\"unknowns\\\":[\\\"whether the same enqueue happens for a row whose head is committed but whose dependencies are missing, which the check does not exercise\\\",\\\"the exact wall-clock grace period in a deployment, since the counter advances per loop iteration rather than per clock tick\\\"],\n \\\"trigger_rationale\\\":\\\"This is the A2 recovery relationship seen from the executor: the enqueue is what hands a blocked instance to the recovery entry, and it is the progress path that the dead rejection branch (C7) depends on.\\\"}\nsub={\\\"action\\\":\\\"check\\\",\\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Closes the last unexercised item of the liveness question: the executor's timeout path is now driven for real (its goroutine runs, the blocked row accumulates the grace period and the instance arrives on the recovery channel). The local construction run reports enqueued true for instance 1.0 after about 12.6 seconds, which both establishes the 